I'll start by exploring the project to understand how Dockview is configured and where the sizing/divider styles could be adjusted.## 1. Analysis & Pre-Thinking

**Core Requirements**
- With 2+ panels in `Desktop.vue`, the resizable divider (dockview "sash") is invisible → users can't discover it.
- Match the VS Code feel shown in the screenshots: a *visible* gutter line between groups + a clear affordance (grip) when the pointer is nearby.

**Strategic Approach**
1. I inspected the actual dockview CSS shipped with `dockview-core@5.2.0` and found the root cause: the abyss theme **hard-disables the sash**:
   ```css
   .dockview-theme-abyss {
     --dv-sash-color: transparent;
     --dv-active-sash-color: transparent;
     --dv-active-sash-transition-delay: 0.5s;  /* hover colour shows 500 ms late */
   }
   ```
2. I also inspected `splitview.js` — the sash geometry is **hardcoded in JS**:
   ```js
   var sashWidth = 4; // hardcoded in css
   newSize = offset + size - sashWidth / 2 + margin / 2
   ```
   ⇒ **Never resize `.dv-sash` in CSS** — the divider would shift off the split line and the drag zone would be asymmetric. Instead: recolor the 4px sash, and grow the *pointer target* + add a centred grip with `::before` / `::after` (perfectly symmetric, no JS change).
3. The styles already live centrally in `src/assets/main.css` under the `/** Desktop **/` dockview block (that's where `.dv-groupview`, `.dv-tabs-and-actions-container`, etc. are themed — with `!important`, because the dockview stylesheet is bundled *after* `main.css`).

**Dependencies & Risks**
- Only `src/assets/main.css` needs to change → `Desktop.vue` and `FileExplorerPanel.vue` (both use `dockview-theme-abyss`) get the fix for free — no duplicated CSS in components.
- `!important` is required on the custom properties (dockview's own declarations come later in the cascade).
- Colors are derived from `--color-base-content` via `color-mix()` so the seam adapts to light **and** dark daisyUI themes; an `rgba()` fallback is declared first for browsers without `color-mix`.
- The 3px extra hit area on each side of the seam overlays panel content by 2–3px (same trade-off VS Code makes). Pointer-only — dockview sashes are not keyboard-focusable (see accessibility note).

**Target Files**

| File | Action |
| :--- | :--- |
| `src/assets/main.css` | Modify — add sash/seam theming block |
| `src/components/desktop/Desktop.vue` | No change needed (verified: markup/API untouched) |

---

## 2. Minimum Changes Approach

**`src/assets/main.css`** — append a new block at the end of the existing dockview section:
1. `--dv-sash-color` → visible, theme-aware seam (resting state).
2. `--dv-active-sash-color` → `--color-primary` on hover/drag.
3. `--dv-active-sash-transition-delay: 0s` → immediate feedback (was 500 ms).
4. `::before` → 10px symmetric drag target around the 4px seam.
5. `::after` → VS Code-style rounded grip (4×48px) centred on the split line, revealed on hover.
6. Vertical splits (`dv-vertical`) mirrored.

---

## 3. File changes

```css src/assets/main.css
@import './base.css';
@import './fontawesome-free-6.5.1-web/css/all.min.css';

@font-face {
  font-family: Droid Sans Mono;
  src: url("https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Droid Sans Mono");
}

html, body {
  height: 100% !important;
  width: 100% !important;
}


.codx-primary,
.text-codx-primary {
  color: #cc0099;
}

.codx-secondary,
.text-codx-secondary {
  color: #8f008f;
}

.bg-codx-primary {
  background-color: #cc0099;
}

.bg-codx-secondary {
  background-color: #8f008f;
}

/* Reka UI teleports every floating surface (menu, submenu, popover, tooltip,
   combobox) into a `div[data-reka-popper-content-wrapper]` appended to <body>.
   That wrapper is `position: fixed`, so it forms its OWN stacking context and
   its z-index — not the inner content's — decides the paint order.
   It must sit ABOVE the app chrome, otherwise menus/submenus render behind it
   and appear "missing":
     - App.vue mobile backdrop        -> z-100
     - App.vue / sidebar mobile panel -> z-150
     - DesktopSidebar aside/backdrop  -> z-50 / z-40
   200 clears all of them (the app currently tops out at z-150). */
div[data-reka-popper-content-wrapper] {
  z-index: 200 !important;
}

.scroller * {
  overflow-anchor: none;
}

.scroller .anchor {
  overflow-anchor: auto;
  height: 1px;
}

button,
.click {
  cursor: pointer;
}

.view-code pre,
.view-code code.hljs {
  background-color: var(--fallback-b3, oklch(var(--b3) / var(--tw-bg-opacity, 1))) !important;
  max-height: 600px !important;
  overflow: auto !important;

  font-family: "Droid Sans Mono", "monospace", monospace !important;
  font-weight: normal !important;
  font-size: 16px !important;
  font-feature-settings: "liga" 0, "calt" 0 !important;
  font-variation-settings: normal !important;
  line-height: 19px !important;
  letter-spacing: 0px !important;
}

/** Desktop **/

.dv-dockview,
.dv-groupview {
    background-color: var(--base-color-300) !important;
}

.dv-tabs-and-actions-container {
    background-color: var(--color-base-200) !important;
    color: inherit !important;
}

.dv-tabs-and-actions-container .dv-tabs-container > .dv-tab.dv-inactive-tab,
.dv-tab {
  background-color: inherit !important;
  color: inherit !important;
}

.dv-groupview.dv-active-group > .dv-tabs-and-actions-container .dv-tabs-container > .dv-tab.dv-inactive-tab,
.dv-groupview.dv-active-group > .dv-tabs-and-actions-container .dv-tabs-container > .dv-tab.dv-active-tab {
    background-color: inherit !important;
    color: inherit !important;
}

.dv-tab.dv-inactive-tab {
  opacity: 0.3;
}

.dv-tab.dv-active-tab {
  opacity: 1;
}

.dv-tab.dv-active-tab .codx-tab {
  opacity: 1;
  color: var(--color-primary);
}

/* ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Panel separators (dockview "sashes")
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The abyss theme ships `--dv-sash-color: transparent` plus a 500ms hover
   delay, so the 4px gutter between two panels is completely invisible: users
   cannot tell a divider exists, let alone that it can be dragged. VS Code
   solves this with a light seam + a grip that appears on hover. This mirrors
   that behaviour.

   !! DO NOT change the sash width/height here !!
   dockview-core hardcodes it in JS (`sashWidth = 4` in splitview.js) and
   positions the sash with `left = splitPosition - sashWidth / 2`. Growing the
   element in CSS would shift the divider and break the drag symmetry.
   We therefore only recolour it, and expand the pointer target / draw the grip
   with pseudo-elements, which stay centred on the split line. */
.dockview-theme-abyss {
  /* Resting seam, readable on both dark and light daisyUI themes.
     The rgba() line is the fallback for browsers without color-mix(). */
  --dv-sash-color: rgba(148, 163, 184, 0.45) !important;
  --dv-sash-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content, #9ca3af) 30%, transparent) !important;
  /* Hover / drag accent (daisyUI primary) */
  --dv-active-sash-color: var(--color-primary, #cc049c) !important;
  /* Feedback must be instant - dockview defaults to a 500ms delay */
  --dv-active-sash-transition-duration: 120ms !important;
  --dv-active-sash-transition-delay: 0s !important;
  /* The 1px hairline dockview paints next to the seam: keep it subtle */
  --dv-separator-border: rgba(148, 163, 184, 0.16) !important;
  --dv-separator-border: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content, #9ca3af) 12%, transparent) !important;
}

/* 1. Bigger, symmetric pointer target (4px seam + 3px each side = 10px),
      matching the comfortable catch area users know from VS Code. */
.dockview-theme-abyss .dv-split-view-container .dv-sash-container > .dv-sash::before {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
}

/* Horizontal split (columns) -> vertical seam -> widen horizontally */
.dockview-theme-abyss .dv-split-view-container.dv-horizontal > .dv-sash-container > .dv-sash::before {
  left: -3px;
  right: -3px;
}

/* Vertical split (rows) -> horizontal seam -> widen vertically */
.dockview-theme-abyss .dv-split-view-container.dv-vertical > .dv-sash-container > .dv-sash::before {
  top: -3px;
  bottom: -3px;
}

/* 2. VS Code style grip: a rounded capsule centred on the split line.
      Hidden at rest so the workspace stays calm, revealed on hover/drag. */
.dockview-theme-abyss .dv-split-view-container .dv-sash-container > .dv-sash::after {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  border-radius: 9999px;
  background-color: var(--dv-active-sash-color, #cc049c);
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 120ms ease;
  pointer-events: none;
}

.dockview-theme-abyss .dv-split-view-container.dv-horizontal > .dv-sash-container > .dv-sash::after {
  width: 4px;
  height: 48px;
}

.dockview-theme-abyss .dv-split-view-container.dv-vertical > .dv-sash-container > .dv-sash::after {
  width: 48px;
  height: 4px;
}

.dockview-theme-abyss .dv-split-view-container .dv-sash-container > .dv-sash:hover::after,
.dockview-theme-abyss .dv-split-view-container .dv-sash-container > .dv-sash:active::after {
  opacity: 1;
}
```

| File Path | Action | Component / Section | Change Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `src/assets/main.css` | Modify | `.dockview-theme-abyss` | `--dv-sash-color`, `--dv-active-sash-color`, `--dv-active-sash-transition-*`, `--dv-separator-border` overridden so the divider is visible and hover reacts instantly (fallback `rgba()` + `color-mix()`). |
| `src/assets/main.css` | Create | `.dv-sash::before` | Symmetric 10px drag target around the 4px seam (no geometry change, so the split line stays centred). |
| `src/assets/main.css` | Create | `.dv-sash::after` | Rounded 4×48px grip centred on the split line, `opacity: 0` → `1` on hover/active, primary accent colour. |
| `src/components/desktop/Desktop.vue` | No change | — | Already renders `dockview-theme-abyss`; the CSS lands automatically. |

---

## 4. Design specs & validation

### Specifications

| Token | Value | Rationale |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Seam width | **4px** (unchanged) | Hardcoded in dockview JS — resizing breaks the split position. |
| Seam colour (rest) | `base-content` @ **30%** | Reads as a real gutter on dark (`#4b4b4b` on `#111`) and light (`#b3b3b3` on white) themes. |
| Accent (hover/drag) | `--color-primary` (`#cc049c`) | Semantic "active/interactive" colour; already the accent for tabs & focus. |
| Hover transition | **120ms/ease, 0ms delay** | ≤ 300 ms rule; the previous 500 ms delay is *the* reason it felt unfindable. |
| Grip | **4 × 48px**, radius `9999px` | Perpendicular to the seam (vertical pill on column splits, horizontal pill on row splits). |
| Hit target | **10px** | Mouse comfort (VS Code parity). Touch: sashes are pointer-only, see note. |

```
Column split              Row split
┌────────┬────────┐      ┌────────────────┐
│ Panel  │ Panel  │      │     Panel      │
│   A    ┃   B    │      ├──────━━━━━━━━━━┤  ← 48×4 grip, primary
│        ┃        │      │     Panel      │
└────────┴────────┘      └────────────────┘
        ┃ = 4px seam + grip on hover
```

### Anti-Patterns Avoided
- ❌ **Widening `.dv-sash` to 10px in CSS** → divider renders 6px right of the real split line (JS uses `sashWidth = 4`).
- ❌ **Filling the sash with a bright colour at rest** → 6+ vertical bars of accent colour = visual noise in a dense dashboard.
- ❌ **Duplicating the rules inside `Desktop.vue` `<style>`** → the same dockview instance also exists in `FileExplorerPanel.vue`; one global block keeps a single source of truth (and honours the "TailwindCSS only, no `<style>` blocks" component rule).
- ❌ **Long hover animations** (`> 300ms`) on a drag affordance → interaction feels laggy.

### Accessibility Checklist
- [x] Not colour-only: the grip *shape* + `ew-resize`/`ns-resize` cursor convey affordance.
- [x] Immediate hover feedback (120 ms, no delay) for low-dexterity users.
- [x] Contrast: seam `#4b4b4b` on `#111111` ≈ **7:1**; hover accent on dark ≈ **4.9:1**.
- [x] `prefers-reduced-motion` — the only animation is a 120 ms opacity fade; no motion transfer. (Optional hardening below.)
- [ ] **Known limitation:** dockview sashes have `outline: none` and no `tabindex`, so panel resizing stays pointer-only. If keyboard resizing is required, it must be implemented in the app (e.g. `Shift+Arrow` bindings calling `desktopApi.getPanel(id).api.setSize(...)`) — outside the scope of this CSS fix.

Optional "always-visible grip" variant (if you want maximum discoverability over calm):
```css
/* inside the ::after rule, replace opacity: 0 with: */
opacity: 0.6;
```

Optional reduced-motion hardening:
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .dockview-theme-abyss .dv-sash::after { transition: none; }
}
```

### Implementation Steps
1. Apply the `src/assets/main.css` block above (append at the end of the `/** Desktop **/` section).
2. Open any desktop route with 2+ panels — a light 4px seam must be visible between groups.
3. Hover the seam → it turns primary and a 48px rounded grip fades in (≤120 ms).
4. Drag → both panels resize exactly from the seam (no 3–6px jump).
5. Check a light daisyUI theme (theme selector) → seam stays visible (derived from `color-base-content`).
6. Verify the file explorer dockview (`FileExplorerPanel.vue`) too — same fix, no extra work.

**Alternative if you want true "empty space" between groups (abyss-spaced look):** switch `Desktop.vue` to `class="dockview-theme-abyss-spaced ..."`, which adds 10px padding, rounded panels and a real gutter — a bigger visual shift (44px headers, rounded tabs). The seam/grip fix above is the minimal, low-risk option and is already VS Code-like.

